          "question": "I get short of breath climbing two flights of stairs but I am completely fine at rest.",
          "answer": "The triage check did not find an emergency pattern in what you described. That is not the same as saying nothing is wrong. Breathlessness that only shows up on exertion can have many causes, and I cannot tell which applies to you.\n\n**Get help right away** if you have any of these: breathlessness at rest or breathlessness that comes on suddenly, chest pain with breathlessness, trouble speaking in full sentences, or blue or gray lips or fingertips. (Source: \"Severe difficulty breathing,\" emergency guidance.)\n\n**If none of those apply**, it is reasonable to book a visit with a doctor or nurse and describe what you told me: how far you can go before it starts, whether it has changed over time, and whether anything else comes with it. The guidance I found does not say what causes exertional breathlessness, so I cannot tell you what it might be.\n\nThe retrieved material also did not cover how to assess this symptom or what tests might be done. Some general notes that did come up:\n\n- Breathing techniques such as breathing out during the hard part of a climb, and leaning forward with your arms supported, are described for managing breathlessness during effort. (Source: \"Staying active with COPD,\" respiratory educational summary.) This is general advice, not a fix for an unknown cause, so check with a clinician before relying on it.\n- Regular activity is generally encouraged, but that guidance is about building fitness and does not address why you are short of breath on stairs. (Source: \"Physical activity for high blood pressure,\" lifestyle guidance.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
